# Papervine rules

## The sidebar is `docs.json`, and nothing else

Papervine never infers structure from folder layout. A page appears where `docs.json` says it
appears, and a page listed nowhere renders at its URL and appears in no sidebar at all.

So **writing a page is two edits**: the `.mdx` file, and the nav entry. The file alone looks
completely fine when you open its URL, which is why the missing entry survives review.

Nav entries are paths from the docs root, **without the extension** — `"guides/auth"` means
`guides/auth.mdx`. The index page is written `"index"`; its route is `/`.

Config file: `docs.json`. Pages: `.mdx` (`.md` renders too). Match the naming already in the
directory; default to kebab-case.

## Frontmatter

```yaml
---
title: "Connect a repository"
description: "Point Papervine at a Git repo of MDX and docs.json, and publish on push."
---
```

| Field | Type | What it changes |
|-------|------|-----------------|
| `title` | string | The `<h1>`, the sidebar label, the browser title. Derived from the slug if absent. |
| `description` | string | The line under the heading — and in search results, `llms.txt`, and social cards. |
| `sidebarTitle` | string | A shorter label, when the real title is too long for the sidebar. |
| `icon` | string | A **Lucide** name, beside the sidebar entry. |
| `tag` | string | A small badge beside the sidebar entry. |
| `hidden` | boolean | Drops it from the sidebar. Still reachable by URL. |
| `noindex` | boolean | Out of search, `llms.txt`, and search engines. |
| `keywords` | string[] | Extra search terms. |
| `url` | string | The sidebar entry points here instead of at the page. |
| `groups` | string[] | Restricts the page to those reader-auth groups. |
| `public` | boolean | Opts the page out of group gating. |

Any key containing a colon (`og:image`, `twitter:card`) becomes a `<meta>` tag, overriding the
site-wide value. Unknown keys are ignored, not errors.

## Inside a page

**Links between your own pages are root-relative, no extension** — `/guides/auth`. Not `../auth`,
not an absolute URL. A site can be served from a subdomain, a custom domain, or a path prefix,
and only the root-relative form survives all three.

**Images live in the repo and are referenced root-relative**, with alt text describing what the
image shows rather than what it is:

```mdx
![The deploy log after a successful sync](/images/deploy-log.png)
```

**Every fence gets a language** — that is what produces highlighting. Text after the language
becomes the block's title.

## House style

- Second person, present tense. "You connect a repo", not "the user connects a repo".
- Say what a thing does before how to configure it. Someone arriving from search needs the
  sentence that orients them.
- Sentence case in headings and code-block titles.
- No emoji, no filler. "Simply", "just", "powerful" and "seamless" all survive deletion.
- Don't repeat the `title` as an `# H1` — the title already renders as the heading.
- Two pages with real titles beat one page with four levels of nesting.

## These don't fail — they just don't work

The renderer is built so one unsupported thing never breaks a page. The cost is that most
mistakes render as *something*, and nothing tells you:

| | |
|---|---|
| `"hidden": true` on a **tab** | Ignored. Mark its groups instead. |
| A Font Awesome or Tabler icon name | Renders nothing at all. Lucide only, or `<Icon src="…" />`. |
| A misspelled component | Renders its children as plain text. |
| A page in no nav group | Reachable only by direct URL. |
| `mode`, `openapi`, `api` in frontmatter | Not read. There is no per-page layout switch, and endpoint pages come from the nav. |
| An anchor with an `href` and no pages | Dropped. Off-site links go in `navbar.links`. |
| A second `versions` / `languages` entry | Only the first renders. |

The one thing that genuinely breaks a site is **invalid JSON in `docs.json`** — usually a
trailing comma. It presents as a site that renders but looks unstyled, which sends people
hunting through CSS instead.

And when a key warns as unsupported: **leave it**. Passing unknown config through is deliberate,
so a repo migrated from another platform keeps rendering. A warning is not a deletion notice.
